{"id": 38, "ts": 1781174086.3796449, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I remain with Candidate\u202fC as my provisional recommendation. James\u2019 update on Candidate\u202fD reinforces my view that D does not yet meet the safety\u2011critical bar. While D \u201cresponds to unexpected events adequately,\u201d the same notes flag a \u201chot temper,\u201d which raises doubts about calmness under pressure\u2014a non\u2011negotiable trait for long\u2011haul flights. Moreover, there is no explicit evidence of operational reliability; we only know he \u201ctakes responsibility seriously\u201d and \u201ccan concentrate very well,\u201d which are positive but insufficient to confirm consistent, dependable behavior. The lack of any concrete data on crew cooperation or professional communication\u2014combined with the observations that D is regarded as a \u201cloner\u201d and \u201cmoody\u201d\u2014further weakens his fit for a collaborative cockpit environment.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fC is documented as **100\u202f% reliable**, **creates a positive atmosphere with his crew**, **keeps calm in a crisis**, and **can make correct decisions quickly**. These facts directly satisfy the core criteria of reliability, crew cooperation, stress resilience, and decision quality. Although C has communication\u2011style concerns (difficulty articulating ideas, egocentric, reluctant to further education), these issues appear less detrimental than the unresolved reliability and teamwork gaps we see with D and the unfriendly, uncooperative traits of A and B.\nTherefore, based on the current evidence, Candidate\u202fC remains the strongest overall fit."}
